{
  "abstract": "We study geometric derivative sampling for the polyanalytic polynomial triangles that arise in quadratic jet determinant arguments. The question is whether explicit global sampling, followed by radial concentration, supplies stronger finite-rank information for discriminant bounds. We reduce the infinite geometric-channel energy to ordinary analytic lattice sampling by an exact double Gaussian identity. An explicit negative-binomial Bessel tail then proves finite-channel frames at every fixed strict large-order margin above the necessary density line, in a specified true-level metric. We give fully directed constants for an elongated quadratic-lattice shape.\n\nThe continuous radial operator admits a fixed two-mode squeeze: its spectral distributions are one-mode gamma laws. Finite triangular compressions are Meixner moment matrices, and every exterior matrix entry has a finite polynomial formula. Exact controls show why functional calculus cannot be moved through the finite projection. A positive generating function computes all triangular trace moments with a number of terms independent of triangle height for fixed moment order. It yields an elementary limiting radial distribution and an asymptotic envelope for the rank certificate.\n\nFor complete lattice disks with fixed sampling parameters and derivative-to-level ratio at least two, the existing lower-support Wronskian theorem asymptotically dominates this envelope, even if the compressed spectrum is computed exactly. This limits the stated certificate rather than actual matrix rank or all Gaussian methods. Classical sampling, amplifier and Meixner tools are explicitly credited. Reproducible exact algebra and directed scalar calculations support the formulas and finite examples. No new discriminant endpoint, finite census, or uniform finite threshold for the asymptotic comparison is claimed.",
